WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.049
Film Courage: So I think you said earlier
that most of the screenplays that you’ve

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:07.960
sold have been through prior working relationships
where you knew the person.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:12.400
But when you did go and pitch because it sounds
like you have done that, when someone said

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.730
“No,” what did that “No” mean?

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.020
Christine Conradt, screenwriter: It meant
that the project wasn’t right for them at

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.770
that time.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:25.650
I think you have to be, you have to have a
lot of confidence in your writing.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.779
First of all to get to that level where you’re
ready to pitch and they are willing to take

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:35.280
a pitch from you are probably at a level where
you know you’re a pretty good writer.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.110
So you can’t let that discourage you.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:43.800
There are projects that we pitched all over
town that I still love and that I still think

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.770
are great projects and no one has bought them
and so it wasn’t the right time for that.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:55.100
You can kind of obsess over that but the best
thing to do is to keep moving on, go to your

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.289
next one, keep moving on.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:01:01.070
You can always revisit something later and
a lot of times when you do revisit it you

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.369
start to see things about it that you didn’t
see before like “Oh, I should have done

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:07.590
things differently or knowing what I know
now I would do this because you were just

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.890
more informed than you were before.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:15.079
So for me you just don’t let it discourage
you, that’s the biggest takeaway for that.

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:21.469
Film Courage: Did you always have that approach
to it or was the first time sort of difficult?

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:24.179
Christine: I think I always had that approach?

00:01:24.179 --> 00:01:28.640
I think my attitude was (and I think I’ve
gotten softer as I’ve gotten older) but

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.259
I think when I was younger I was like “Well,
if they don’t like it, forget them.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:37.529
I’m moving on to the next person.

00:01:37.529 --> 00:01:40.069
Like if they don’t get it, someone else
will.”

00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:44.380
But I think that’s the right attitude to
have.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:53.060
And I think that you can internalize a lot
of things as a writer and I think writers

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.499
tend to.

00:01:54.499 --> 00:01:56.799
We’re always looking for “What did I do
wrong?

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:58.529
What could I do better?

00:01:58.529 --> 00:02:00.429
Why isn’t this working for me?”

00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:04.880
And I think that if you just stay true to
what you believe in, eventually you’re going

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.570
to find that audience that is going to connect
and that producer and maybe it’s just the

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:14.110
timing, maybe you haven’t met them yet but
again it goes to why networking is so important.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.890
Expanding that group of people and networking
and finding that group of people that you

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:22.360
want to work with who creatively align with
you is really, really important.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.371
Film Courage: Yeah and the interesting thing
is too that so many things change behind the

00:02:26.371 --> 00:02:30.230
scenes whatever part of the industry that
you’re in and you will never know why and

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.700
you’ll never know “Is it me?

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.700
What was it?

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.700
Did I say the wrong thing?”

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.799
Christine: I tell actors this all the time
from a director’s standpoint when they do

00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:45.360
auditions and they have five minutes to do
their read, we know the moment they walk in

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.360
if it’s a “No.”

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.360
Film Courage: Wow.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.409
Christine: And I tell actors all the time
don’t obsess all the time over what you

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:52.950
should of said differently or how you should
have delivered a line.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.270
It’s not even about that.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:59.659
We either connected you to that character
or we didn’t, period.

00:02:59.659 --> 00:03:02.060
And it’s sort of the same way with writing.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.560
You are going to find people who (at one point)
this is the project.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.859
Someone loves this project and it’s what
they want to do and they get it.

00:03:09.859 --> 00:03:11.770
But there’s going to be lots of people who
don’t.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.069
That’s why there is lots of stuff on television.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:17.020
That’s why there’s lots of movies playing
at any given time.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:21.549
There’s not one thing for everybody, so
don’t take “No” for an answer, just

00:03:21.549 --> 00:03:22.549
keep moving.

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:23.430
Film Courage: I like that.

